  addressing the same build and then  running that docker image  down the road along with the tarball and  the condo lock as well however i will  point out that the condo lock  probably won't work down the road  because of  how condo channels work and that the  packages may no longer exist at that  point in time  additionally  you can see the full logs of the conda  the build that actually took place  so really quick how would a user create  a new environment so you just click  create environment you can drag and drop  new environments in here the other thing  too um  we can do this and then  flask  and then we see that it was added and as  soon as it was added we already see that  a build was queued and very soon  we'll see that it's actually building  so you can see how it has it will  automatically start to build these  environments  other things that i wanted to point out  was that  it also builds these environments on the  file system  so i mentioned a little bit about having  a jupiter lab extension for installing  conda environments  but the big benefit here is that you can  see that it's automatically added  via content in b kernels  and we can for instance play with this  is the data science environment that was  just created  so  if we look at data science  we will see  we have  numpy installed  so we can import numpy but if we import  number  that doesn't exist and we can then  quickly trigger a new build by just  editing this  and adding number  the other thing that you'll notice with  this though too  is that we keep track of all of our  previous builds so we have a nice way  of keeping track of our environments  over time as well  i mentioned a little bit about  how there is role-based access  behind all of this so right now i'm  currently authenticated and the way i  have this user authenticated is you can  see all environments  but let's suppose that i log out  you'll see that as soon as i log out  not all the environments are available  to me and that's because by default  the default namespace is read only  and then there are abilities that as  soon as you log in this is going  integrations with jupiterhub for  authentication  and as soon as you're logged in you now  get to see all of the namespaces so  there's there's really rich controls on  environments how they're created and  deleted  so i know that was a really fast um  walkthrough of a lot of the things in  condo store but i just want to make sure  i  hit on all those features there  and i may be cutting this a little bit  short with the full presentation if  there are time limits  so i wanted to talk really quickly about  how does condestor actually do the  builds  so we have a sort of environment  that's kind of just a pointer to  whatever the current  build is for that given environment  and then we have a bunch of successive  builds that are tied to environment.yaml  files  and you'll notice that you can have  multiple builds for the same  specification  and this is because  if you have an environment.yaml that's  not pinned down  it could happen that numpy comes up with  a newer version and if you do another  um another build you'll actually get a  newer version of numpy in in the next  build so  it's important to keep track of all the  different versions through time  so here i just wanted to hit on some of  the the pain points that conjustor is  really aiming to solve here  so the first one that i mentioned a  little bit before was it and user  friction this is something we  saw over and over when working with  clients  and  that is that all of our end users maybe  data scientists or  just regular developers they they need a  given package and it's really hard to  understand why it can't have that  package  available  now  and then on the other side when you get  to see the it's perspective you realize  like the complexity behind updating  environments  and  there's a lot of method to the madness  on you know why it takes such a long  time and really both sides are really  frustrated with the process  so the things that i i really think that  condustor can bring here is that it has  the rich rbac controls that will allow  it to have like dedicated read-only  environments that users can't touch  but at the same time allowing users to  have their own playground environments  but still have restrictions on you can  only have you know these sorts of  packages you can only install from this  dedicated artifactory channel  all of these constraints that it might  want to limit on their users  um yeah and then i mentioned here the  ability to enforce constraints  another key issue that i just wanted to  make sure that i i touched on here was  the ability to productionize  environments  so very often we'd see  some users just developing on jupiter  lab  they come up with this awesome notebook  that solves some  problem but the issue is that they need  this to be run every week for the next  five years  and it needs to be run on a regular  basis well as soon as they have that  notebook in that environment they really  kind of just hand it off to it and say  you know it's your job to make sure this  runs  forever essentially  and  that's a really tough problem and i  think one of the key issues there  is that we don't have a way to  reproducibly identify the environment  that the notebook was developed in and  somehow mirror that when we're running  that  on a workflow on a regular basis  so how condos store helps this is that  every build is uniquely accessible it's  available as a condo pack docker image  lock file  pendiaml  i i have ideas where i'd really like to  start producing isos and also  for the hpc community  it's really escaping me right now but  their their packaging format i'd really  like to produce singularity containers  as well there's really no reason we  couldn't do that  um like i mentioned all the builds  output many different forms  um  additionally all environments are  artifact  all artifacts are put in s3  and this is a really important thing to  have affordable storage and also just a  really easy way to back up  all of the  environments and builds that were  created  and then finally anybody that's ever  done a conda update  maybe many times has found a time when  you do a condo update and it gets an  inconsistent solve  and you run into issues there so condo  store actually does a complete new  install every single time  and it creates a so kind of sacrifices a  little bit of space  for that really reproducible  reproducible aspect there  and then this is a feature that i really  care about that i wanted to at least  show  i got really motivated and mixed there  is a project called mixery  and it is kind of what i would call an  on-the-fly docker container builder  and i was determined to build this for  conda  so  i already showed here how i pulled an  existing image but let's do a docker run  minus it  so we'll contact your same docker  registry but i have a  special conda store dynamic and anything  after this it will build a docker image  with those packages installed  so maybe i want python  less than  uh less than  3.9  i went numpy and then i went flask  installed  so you'll notice really quickly i ran  this and  it doesn't yet have the docker image  because it doesn't really exist and if  we check really quickly on here  and we refresh we'll see that there's  automatically an environment being built  and it's configurable so you can make  this hidden so users don't see it  but my main point here is that this  allows in a kubernetes environment  we can kind of stop creating these like  i mean everybody's had to write docker  files before that seem overly repetitive  and this would allow on-the-fly docker  environments with arbitrary conda  packages installed  you can also configure the the base  image that's used  but i think for really light things that  don't really have any secret sauce in  them i'll give an example we're trying  to run a